1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:02,519
Watch what happens after the truth comes out.

2
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:05,999
The evidence is public. The verdict is in.

3
00:00:06,619 --> 00:00:09,800
And somewhere, someone who lost everything is still

4
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:12,399
finding reasons to defend the person who took

5
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:15,739
it from them. This isn't loyalty. It isn't

6
00:00:15,819 --> 00:00:19,219
stupidity. It's one of the most predictable mechanisms

7
00:00:19,279 --> 00:00:21,939
in the human mind. And once you understand

8
00:00:21,979 --> 00:00:24,859
it, you'll start seeing it everywhere. This is

9
00:00:24,959 --> 00:00:28,000
Shadow Figure. Here's the uncomfortable starting point.

10
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:31,120
The more something has already cost you, your

11
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:34,460
time, your money, your reputation, your

12
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:36,740
sense of who you are, the harder it

13
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:39,440
becomes to admit that the cost was for

14
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:42,800
nothing. Psychologists call the discomfort of holding

15
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:46,240
two conflicting beliefs at once cognitive dissonance.

16
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:50,240
You believe this person was trustworthy. You defended

17
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:53,020
that belief to your family, staked your savings

18
00:00:53,080 --> 00:00:55,860
on it, maybe staked your identity as someone

19
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:59,380
who isn't easily fooled. Then new evidence arrives

20
00:00:59,660 --> 00:01:02,420
that the belief was wrong. Two things can't

21
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,440
both be true. Either you were deceived or

22
00:01:05,500 --> 00:01:08,680
the deception isn't real, and the mind, given

23
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:11,440
a choice between those two, does not neutrally

24
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:14,840
weigh the facts. It reaches for whichever conclusion

25
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:18,300
costs it less. Accepting "I was deceived"

26
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:21,500
means rewriting your own story. You have to

27
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:24,560
admit you handed over money, trust, or years

28
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:27,510
of your life based on something false, and

29
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:29,180
you have to sit with what that says

30
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:32,300
about your own judgment. Rejecting the new

31
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,660
evidence costs nothing right now. It only costs

32
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,520
you later. So the mind, in the moment,

33
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:41,880
very often takes the cheaper option. It

34
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:44,660
reinterprets the new evidence instead of the old

35
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:47,580
belief. It finds a reason the journalist is

36
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:51,020
biased, the whistleblower is jealous, the numbers are

37
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:54,100
being misrepresented, not because the person is

38
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:57,220
unintelligent, because the alternative is more

39
00:01:57,260 --> 00:02:00,700
expensive to carry. There's a second layer underneath

40
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:04,130
this, specific to relationships rather than beliefs,

41
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:08,020
trauma bonding. When affection and harm

42
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:10,960
arrive in an unpredictable alternating cycle,

43
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:14,800
genuine warmth, then a violation, then genuine

44
00:02:14,860 --> 00:02:18,080
warmth again, the nervous system doesn't file that

45
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,380
as this relationship has a problem. It

46
00:02:21,440 --> 00:02:24,400
files the relief that follows each bad episode

47
00:02:24,480 --> 00:02:27,860
as intense relationship because the brain's chemistry

48
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,640
treats the alternating pattern of tension and relief

49
00:02:31,680 --> 00:02:35,120
as unusually significant. The bond doesn't form

50
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:38,820
despite the harm. It forms partly because of

51
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:41,620
the pattern the harm creates. Both

52
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:45,020
layers share the same underlying shape. The

53
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:48,380
mind is not a neutral courtroom weighing evidence.

54
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:51,420
It is closer to a defense attorney that

55
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:54,597
already has a client, the client being the

56
00:02:54,676 --> 00:02:57,916
version of events you've already committed to, and

57
00:02:57,976 --> 00:03:00,556
it will work harder to protect that client

58
00:03:00,937 --> 00:03:03,156
the more expensive the client's guilt would be

59
00:03:03,216 --> 00:03:06,316
to accept. And this mechanism doesn't only

60
00:03:06,376 --> 00:03:09,236
appear after the fact, waiting to be exploited

61
00:03:09,296 --> 00:03:12,776
by accident. Skilled manipulators build it in from

62
00:03:12,816 --> 00:03:15,937
the start deliberately. They ask for small

63
00:03:15,977 --> 00:03:19,277
commitments early, a favor, a loan, a

64
00:03:19,336 --> 00:03:22,457
public endorsement, an introduction to your friends,

65
00:03:22,916 --> 00:03:26,316
specifically because each small commitment raises the

66
00:03:26,376 --> 00:03:29,156
cost of later doubt. By the time real

67
00:03:29,216 --> 00:03:32,636
warning signs appear, the victim isn't just deciding

68
00:03:32,736 --> 00:03:35,816
whether to trust a stranger. They're deciding whether

69
00:03:35,876 --> 00:03:38,556
months or years of their own choices were

70
00:03:38,617 --> 00:03:42,036
a mistake, and that second question is always

71
00:03:42,097 --> 00:03:45,296
harder to answer honestly than the first. This

72
00:03:45,336 --> 00:03:47,836
is also not limited to dramatic cases of

73
00:03:47,896 --> 00:03:51,396
fraud or abuse. The same mechanism runs

74
00:03:51,497 --> 00:03:54,976
quietly in ordinary situations. Defending a

75
00:03:55,056 --> 00:03:58,356
bad hire you personally championed, staying in a

76
00:03:58,436 --> 00:04:01,476
job that stopped making sense because you already

77
00:04:01,516 --> 00:04:04,696
turned down other offers, insisting a purchase was

78
00:04:04,756 --> 00:04:07,597
worth it after you've already spent the money.

79
00:04:07,656 --> 00:04:10,916
The stakes are smaller, but the wiring underneath

80
00:04:10,976 --> 00:04:13,997
is identical. You've already seen a version of

81
00:04:14,077 --> 00:04:16,937
this on this channel. In our episode on

82
00:04:16,997 --> 00:04:19,797
Elizabeth Holmes and Theranos, a young

83
00:04:19,857 --> 00:04:22,838
employee named Tyler Shultz raised serious

84
00:04:22,897 --> 00:04:25,997
concerns about the company's blood testing technology

85
00:04:26,377 --> 00:04:29,217
in twenty fourteen. His own grandfather,

86
00:04:29,658 --> 00:04:33,137
George Shultz, a former US Secretary of State

87
00:04:33,197 --> 00:04:36,418
who sat on Theranos's board, reportedly took the

88
00:04:36,498 --> 00:04:39,717
company's side over his own grandson's for a

89
00:04:39,777 --> 00:04:43,117
significant stretch of time, a rift serious

90
00:04:43,177 --> 00:04:46,118
enough to strain the family for years. Think

91
00:04:46,217 --> 00:04:48,918
about what that costed George Shultz to consider

92
00:04:48,997 --> 00:04:52,258
his grandson right. It meant admitting his own

93
00:04:52,357 --> 00:04:55,438
judgment, built over a long and decorated

94
00:04:55,497 --> 00:04:58,857
career reading people and institutions, had

95
00:04:58,918 --> 00:05:02,117
been wrong about someone he'd vouched for. It's

96
00:05:02,177 --> 00:05:04,497
not that the evidence wasn't available to him.

97
00:05:05,078 --> 00:05:07,297
It's that the evidence was more expensive to

98
00:05:07,418 --> 00:05:10,717
accept than to set aside. This is the

99
00:05:10,857 --> 00:05:14,297
same mechanism, just wearing a different relationship.

100
00:05:14,797 --> 00:05:17,557
Not a romantic partner, not a cult leader,

101
00:05:18,098 --> 00:05:21,397
but a grandfather whose own credibility was

102
00:05:21,477 --> 00:05:23,897
quietly tied to the company being what it

103
00:05:23,937 --> 00:05:26,924
claimed to be. The same pattern shows up

104
00:05:27,184 --> 00:05:30,524
almost mechanically whenever a fraud is exposed

105
00:05:30,904 --> 00:05:33,344
and investors are asked what they plan to

106
00:05:33,384 --> 00:05:36,854
do next. A striking number don't immediately

107
00:05:36,924 --> 00:05:40,174
withdraw. Some publicly defend the person at the

108
00:05:40,204 --> 00:05:43,024
center of the scandal well past the point

109
00:05:43,064 --> 00:05:46,304
where the evidence is settled. Psychologist Leon

110
00:05:46,364 --> 00:05:49,684
Festinger first documented this pattern decades

111
00:05:49,764 --> 00:05:52,964
ago, studying a small group that had predicted

112
00:05:52,984 --> 00:05:55,704
the end of the world and continued believing

113
00:05:56,064 --> 00:05:59,184
even after the date passed without incident. Some

114
00:05:59,224 --> 00:06:02,144
of them, if anything, became more convinced

115
00:06:02,244 --> 00:06:05,604
afterward, not less. Members who had quit their

116
00:06:05,644 --> 00:06:09,044
jobs, given away possessions, and told their families

117
00:06:09,124 --> 00:06:11,224
the world was ending had the most to

118
00:06:11,284 --> 00:06:14,484
lose by admitting the prediction failed. And it

119
00:06:14,544 --> 00:06:17,524
was precisely those members, the ones who had

120
00:06:17,544 --> 00:06:20,764
paid the highest price, who clung hardest to

121
00:06:20,804 --> 00:06:24,244
the belief once it was disproven. Members

122
00:06:24,284 --> 00:06:27,084
who had made smaller sacrifices found it much

123
00:06:27,224 --> 00:06:30,704
easier to walk away quietly. The prediction had

124
00:06:30,724 --> 00:06:34,224
cost them too much—reputations, relationships,

125
00:06:34,584 --> 00:06:37,484
money—for simply admitting error to feel

126
00:06:37,544 --> 00:06:40,824
survivable. So belief adjusted to protect the

127
00:06:40,844 --> 00:06:43,784
investment instead of the investment being written

128
00:06:43,864 --> 00:06:47,114
off to protect accurate belief. That's the

129
00:06:47,144 --> 00:06:49,584
same math playing out in a boardroom, a

130
00:06:49,704 --> 00:06:52,464
courtroom gallery, or a family dinner table.

131
00:06:53,164 --> 00:06:55,284
The size of the sunk cost predicts the

132
00:06:55,344 --> 00:06:58,424
size of the denial far more reliably than

133
00:06:58,464 --> 00:07:01,544
the strength of the new evidence does. What

134
00:07:01,584 --> 00:07:04,784
made Festinger's group especially striking is that the

135
00:07:04,824 --> 00:07:07,344
failed prophecy didn't just fail to shake their

136
00:07:07,404 --> 00:07:10,724
belief. For the most committed members, it produced

137
00:07:10,784 --> 00:07:13,404
a burst of new recruiting effort right afterward.

138
00:07:14,124 --> 00:07:16,604
Having just absorbed the largest possible blow to

139
00:07:16,644 --> 00:07:19,444
the belief, they didn't retreat from it. They

140
00:07:19,504 --> 00:07:21,864
needed it to be true more urgently than

141
00:07:21,944 --> 00:07:25,044
before, and convincing others became a way of

142
00:07:25,124 --> 00:07:28,091
reconfirming it to themselves. Our very first

143
00:07:28,171 --> 00:07:31,131
episode covered Bernie Madoff, whose fraud ran for

144
00:07:31,171 --> 00:07:33,531
thirty years before collapsing in two thousand and

145
00:07:33,572 --> 00:07:36,891
eight. Even after his arrest was public, after

146
00:07:36,931 --> 00:07:39,271
his own sons had turned him in, after

147
00:07:39,331 --> 00:07:42,811
the confession had been reported everywhere, some investors

148
00:07:42,891 --> 00:07:44,712
still held out hope that at least part

149
00:07:44,771 --> 00:07:47,051
of their money was real, that surely a

150
00:07:47,112 --> 00:07:50,511
man that respected, that embedded in their community

151
00:07:50,551 --> 00:07:53,771
and their synagogues and their charity boards couldn't

152
00:07:53,831 --> 00:07:57,271
have been lying to them personally, specifically for

153
00:07:57,351 --> 00:08:00,831
decades. That reaction wasn't about the strength of

154
00:08:00,871 --> 00:08:04,231
the evidence against Madoff. The evidence by that

155
00:08:04,312 --> 00:08:07,711
point was total. It was about what accepting

156
00:08:07,751 --> 00:08:10,771
that evidence would have meant, that trusted friends

157
00:08:10,831 --> 00:08:14,071
had personally recommended a fraud, that decades of

158
00:08:14,132 --> 00:08:17,271
comfortable retirement planning had been fiction, that

159
00:08:17,351 --> 00:08:20,851
people who prided themselves on financial sophistication

160
00:08:20,911 --> 00:08:23,451
had been completely and repeatedly wrong.

161
00:08:24,251 --> 00:08:27,231
For some, disbelief lasted longer than it should

162
00:08:27,331 --> 00:08:30,471
have, not from lack of intelligence, but

163
00:08:30,531 --> 00:08:33,731
because the alternative required rewriting their own

164
00:08:33,811 --> 00:08:36,512
history. Our episode on Peter

165
00:08:36,612 --> 00:08:39,792
Popoff shows the same reflex in a different

166
00:08:39,872 --> 00:08:43,131
arena entirely. Even after James Randi's

167
00:08:43,211 --> 00:08:46,571
exposure aired on national television, a hidden

168
00:08:46,652 --> 00:08:49,851
earpiece, a wife feeding him information from

169
00:08:49,911 --> 00:08:52,832
cards filled out minutes earlier, some

170
00:08:52,851 --> 00:08:56,311
congregation members who had personally felt healed

171
00:08:56,371 --> 00:08:59,471
by Popoff continued attending his services.

172
00:09:00,051 --> 00:09:03,071
Accepting the exposure meant accepting that a moment

173
00:09:03,112 --> 00:09:06,351
they'd experienced as deeply personally spiritual

174
00:09:06,671 --> 00:09:09,791
had a mundane explanation. For some, that was

175
00:09:09,832 --> 00:09:12,391
simply a more expensive thing to believe than

176
00:09:12,431 --> 00:09:15,831
the alternative, that the tape itself was somehow

177
00:09:15,851 --> 00:09:19,112
misleading. Here's why this matters beyond

178
00:09:19,232 --> 00:09:22,472
other people's stories. This mechanism runs in you

179
00:09:22,512 --> 00:09:25,812
too, right now, in whatever you currently believe

180
00:09:25,892 --> 00:09:28,272
strongly and have already paid a price to

181
00:09:28,332 --> 00:09:31,652
believe. The clearest sign you're inside it is

182
00:09:31,712 --> 00:09:34,892
a specific kind of mental motion, noticing that

183
00:09:34,932 --> 00:09:38,152
you're working harder to explain away new evidence

184
00:09:38,552 --> 00:09:41,112
than you ever worked to originally verify the

185
00:09:41,152 --> 00:09:44,112
claim you're defending. If you spent five minutes

186
00:09:44,152 --> 00:09:47,212
deciding to trust someone and you're now spending

187
00:09:47,272 --> 00:09:50,092
five hours constructing reasons the

188
00:09:50,172 --> 00:09:52,992
accusations against them don't count, that

189
00:09:53,052 --> 00:09:55,452
asymmetry is itself the signal,

190
00:09:56,012 --> 00:09:58,172
regardless of who turns out to be right.

191
00:09:58,632 --> 00:10:01,592
A second sign specific to close relationships,

192
00:10:02,372 --> 00:10:04,532
if the moments you remember as proof of

193
00:10:04,592 --> 00:10:07,972
how much someone loves you are disproportionately moments

194
00:10:08,052 --> 00:10:10,592
that came right after they hurt you, that's

195
00:10:10,652 --> 00:10:13,462
not evidence of a strong bond. That's the

196
00:10:13,552 --> 00:10:16,992
shape of trauma bonding, relief being mistaken

197
00:10:17,032 --> 00:10:20,132
for love, because relief is what shows up

198
00:10:20,172 --> 00:10:23,531
right after the pain the same person caused.

199
00:10:23,562 --> 00:10:26,092
The practical habit that cuts through both versions

200
00:10:26,152 --> 00:10:28,912
of this is the same one. Take yourself,

201
00:10:29,212 --> 00:10:32,472
the specific person, and the specific relationship out

202
00:10:32,512 --> 00:10:35,392
of the sentence and describe only the actions.

203
00:10:35,912 --> 00:10:38,152
Not, "He loves me. He just has a

204
00:10:38,212 --> 00:10:41,442
temper." Instead, a person did this specific

205
00:10:41,532 --> 00:10:44,852
thing, then this specific thing on this specific

206
00:10:44,892 --> 00:10:48,152
timeline. Then ask what you'd tell a stranger

207
00:10:48,192 --> 00:10:50,672
who described those actions to you with no

208
00:10:50,812 --> 00:10:54,262
names attached and no history to protect. The

209
00:10:54,332 --> 00:10:56,872
version of you giving advice to a stranger

210
00:10:56,952 --> 00:10:59,552
has access to a clarity that the version

211
00:10:59,592 --> 00:11:02,172
of you living inside the sunk cost does

212
00:11:02,232 --> 00:11:05,032
not. This is also why outside

213
00:11:05,132 --> 00:11:08,452
intervention matters so much in these situations.

214
00:11:08,952 --> 00:11:11,232
Not because the people caught inside a fraud

215
00:11:11,552 --> 00:11:14,732
or an abusive relationship are foolish, but

216
00:11:14,772 --> 00:11:18,072
because the mechanism specifically targets the part of

217
00:11:18,112 --> 00:11:20,932
the mind that would otherwise raise the alarm.

218
00:11:21,612 --> 00:11:24,712
A friend, a therapist, a journalist, a

219
00:11:24,792 --> 00:11:28,252
regulator, anyone standing outside the sunk cost

220
00:11:28,772 --> 00:11:32,102
can see the pattern precisely because they haven't

221
00:11:32,132 --> 00:11:34,892
paid the price that makes seeing it expensive.

222
00:11:35,461 --> 00:11:38,032
There's also a timing element worth naming

223
00:11:38,052 --> 00:11:41,492
directly. The earlier you catch this in yourself,

224
00:11:41,932 --> 00:11:44,488
the cheaper it is to act on. Every

225
00:11:44,568 --> 00:11:47,348
additional week of defending a decision adds to

226
00:11:47,408 --> 00:11:50,129
the sunk cost, which adds to the resistance

227
00:11:50,448 --> 00:11:53,408
the next piece of evidence will face. This

228
00:11:53,468 --> 00:11:55,548
is why people who eventually do leave a

229
00:11:55,608 --> 00:11:58,009
bad relationship or pull their money from a

230
00:11:58,108 --> 00:12:00,808
fraud so often say they wish they'd trusted

231
00:12:00,848 --> 00:12:03,788
their doubts months earlier. Not because the doubts

232
00:12:03,848 --> 00:12:06,808
were new, but because each week of silencing

233
00:12:06,868 --> 00:12:10,368
them made the eventual reckoning more expensive, not

234
00:12:10,449 --> 00:12:13,508
less. None of this means you should distrust

235
00:12:13,589 --> 00:12:16,888
everyone or treat every relationship as a potential

236
00:12:16,949 --> 00:12:20,288
fraud. It means treating your own certainty with

237
00:12:20,348 --> 00:12:23,408
a little suspicion in direct proportion to how

238
00:12:23,468 --> 00:12:25,968
much it has already cost you. The more

239
00:12:26,048 --> 00:12:29,068
you've already put in, the more carefully, not

240
00:12:29,109 --> 00:12:31,589
the more loosely, the next piece of evidence

241
00:12:31,648 --> 00:12:35,008
deserves to be examined. It also means being

242
00:12:35,068 --> 00:12:37,528
more patient with other people caught in this

243
00:12:37,589 --> 00:12:41,008
pattern than you might instinctively be. From the

244
00:12:41,088 --> 00:12:44,088
outside, it's tempting to ask, "How could they

245
00:12:44,128 --> 00:12:46,568
not see it?" As if the answer were

246
00:12:46,649 --> 00:12:48,789
a lack of information or a lack of

247
00:12:48,848 --> 00:12:51,908
intelligence. From the inside, the person

248
00:12:52,028 --> 00:12:54,868
usually has seen fragments of it. They've just

249
00:12:54,948 --> 00:12:58,048
calculated, without fully realizing they're

250
00:12:58,109 --> 00:13:01,228
calculating, that facing it costs more than living

251
00:13:01,348 --> 00:13:04,408
around it. Judgment rarely helps someone move

252
00:13:04,469 --> 00:13:07,888
faster through that math. A calm, repeated,

253
00:13:08,008 --> 00:13:11,308
low-pressure statement of what you're observing, without

254
00:13:11,388 --> 00:13:13,868
ultimatums, without demanding they act

255
00:13:13,908 --> 00:13:17,268
immediately, tends to do more because it doesn't

256
00:13:17,348 --> 00:13:19,988
add another cost to the ledger they're already

257
00:13:20,068 --> 00:13:22,897
avoiding. Every case on this channel so far

258
00:13:23,177 --> 00:13:25,817
has had a moment like this, a point

259
00:13:26,057 --> 00:13:28,597
where the truth was already visible and the

260
00:13:28,718 --> 00:13:31,577
only thing standing between the people involved and

